<article class="prose hiw">
  <h1 id="how-it-works">How thicket works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    thicket looks for two things. One is code that got written more than once. The other is
    modules that import each other in a circle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finding repeats is easy. A mid-sized repo has hundreds of them. What matters is picking the
    20 to 50 worth fixing, because the report goes to a coding agent, and its attention is
    limited. So most of this page is about how thicket picks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The demos run a simplified version of the real thing in your browser. Poke at them. Edit the
    code and see what changes.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#pieces">1. Cut the code into pieces</a>
    <a href="#fingerprints">2. Fingerprint each piece</a>
    <a href="#near">3. Catch the near copies</a>
    <a href="#nesting">4. Drop the nested repeats</a>
    <a href="#ranking">5. Rank what is left</a>
    <a href="#drift">6. Ask if merging buys anything</a>
    <a href="#not-duplication">7. Skip what only looks duplicated</a>
    <a href="#lanes">8. Give tests and types their own sections</a>
    <a href="#varies">9. Say what changes between copies</a>
    <a href="#tangles">10. Untangle the imports</a>
  </nav>

  <!-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="pieces">1. Cut the code into pieces</h2>
  <p>
    thicket parses every file with the TypeScript compiler. That gives it an
    <strong>AST</strong>, or abstract syntax tree: the code as a tree of nodes, one for each
    piece of syntax. A function is a node. So are a loop, a variable name, and even
    the <code>*</code> in <code>a * b</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    thicket walks that tree. Every node that is big enough becomes a <strong>fragment</strong>.
    A fragment is that node plus everything underneath it. That can be a function, a block, a
    loop, an <code>if</code>, or an object literal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fragments nest. The function is one fragment, and the loop inside it is another. Hover over
    the code to see them.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="frag-demo">
    <pre class="frag-code"><code><span class="frag" data-kind="FunctionDeclaration">export function normalize(points: Point[]): Point[] <span class="frag" data-kind="Block">{
  const result: Point[] = [];
  <span class="frag" data-kind="ForOfStatement">for (const p of points) <span class="frag" data-kind="Block">{
    const dx = p.x - ORIGIN.x;
    const dy = p.y - ORIGIN.y;
    const len = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    <span class="frag" data-kind="IfStatement">if (len === 0) <span class="frag" data-kind="Block">{
      result.push({ x: 0, y: 0 });
      continue;
    }</span></span>
    result.push({ x: dx / len, y: dy / len });
  }</span></span>
  return result;
}</span></span></code></pre>
    <figcaption id="frag-label">Hover over the code. Each outline is one fragment.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    "Big enough" means at least 15 AST nodes and 4 lines by default. <code>--depth</code> raises or
    lowers both at once, and <code>--min-nodes</code> and <code>--min-lines</code> set them one
    at a time. Imports, exports and parameter lists never become
    fragments. They look alike in every file, and you can't extract them into anything.
  </p>

  <!-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="fingerprints">2. Fingerprint each piece</h2>
  <p>
    To compare fragments, thicket flattens each one into a list. It walks the fragment's part of
    the tree, top to bottom, and writes down what kind of node each one is, like
    <code>BinaryExpression</code> or <code>PropertyAccessExpression</code>. Each child goes
    inside its own pair of parentheses, so the list keeps the shape of the tree. At the leaves
    it writes the actual name or value.
    Whitespace and comments aren't in the tree, so they never show up.
  </p>
  <p>Here's <code>item.price * item.qty</code>. Hover over a node to find it in the list.</p>

  <figure class="demo" id="ast-demo">
    <div class="ast-wrap">
      <svg class="ast-tree" viewBox="0 0 700 190" role="img" aria-label="The syntax tree of item.price * item.qty"></svg>
      <div class="fp-mode" role="group" aria-label="Show list as">
        <span>Show the list as</span>
        <button type="button" data-ast="l0" aria-pressed="true">L0</button>
        <button type="button" data-ast="l1">L1</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ast-stream"></div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>L1 swaps names for numbered placeholders. More on that just below.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Then thicket boils that list down to a short fingerprint, a hash. It makes two of them, at
    two levels it calls L0 and L1. Fragments with the same hash count as the same code.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>L0</strong> keeps everything, every name and every value. Two fragments match at L0
      when they are the same code, give or take formatting.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>L1</strong> renames things. The first name in the fragment becomes
      <code>v0</code>, the next new one becomes <code>v1</code>, and so on. Strings and numbers
      lose their values and keep only their kind. Two fragments match at L1 when they are the
      same code with different names.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fragments that match form a <strong>finding</strong>. That's one entry in the report.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it below. Edit either side, or pick an example. This demo works on tokens
    instead of AST nodes, so that you can type into it. A token is one word, name, number or bit
    of punctuation, the way you'd type it. It's a simplified version of the real list, but the
    matching works the same way.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="fp-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Examples">
      <button type="button" data-preset="format">Reformatted</button>
      <button type="button" data-preset="renamed">Renamed</button>
      <button type="button" data-preset="constant">Changed a number</button>
      <button type="button" data-preset="labels">Label maps</button>
      <button type="button" data-preset="extra">Added a line</button>
      <button type="button" data-preset="different">Different code</button>
    </div>
    <div class="fp-sides">
      <div class="fp-side">
        <label for="fp-a">Copy A</label>
        <textarea id="fp-a" spellcheck="false" rows="7"></textarea>
        <div class="fp-stream" id="fp-a-stream" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="fp-side">
        <label for="fp-b">Copy B</label>
        <textarea id="fp-b" spellcheck="false" rows="7"></textarea>
        <div class="fp-stream" id="fp-b-stream" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fp-mode" role="group" aria-label="Show tokens as">
      <span>Show tokens as</span>
      <button type="button" data-mode="l0">L0</button>
      <button type="button" data-mode="l1" aria-pressed="true">L1</button>
      <button type="button" data-mode="l2">names ignored (near copies)</button>
    </div>
    <div class="verdict" id="fp-verdict" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <figcaption>
      Simplified. The real list comes from the syntax tree, so it has the parentheses this one
      leaves out. thicket also estimates the near-copy score (section 3), where this demo
      computes it exactly. For code this short, you get the same answer.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Property names get renamed too.</strong> To the syntax tree, <code>p.x</code> is
      two names. So <code>p.x</code> and <code>q.y</code> match at L1.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every fragment starts counting at <code>v0</code>.</strong> If the numbering ran
      across the whole file, a function's names would depend on whatever came before it. Two
      identical functions in different files would get different numbers and never match.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data-heavy fragments sit out L1.</strong> Try the <em>Label maps</em> example. At L1,
      two lookup tables of strings look identical, but they're really two different tables that
      happen to share a format. So if strings and numbers make up more than 35% of a
      fragment's names and values, it can only match at L0. On one real app, this one rule kept a 5-entry label map
      from matching 428 other little string maps and ranking first.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="near">3. Catch the near copies</h2>
  <p>
    Exact hashes have a blind spot. Add one line to one copy, and that copy stops matching the
    rest. A template pasted 24 times, with an extra field on five of them, shows up as two
    unrelated findings: 19 copies and 5 copies.
  </p>
  <p>
    So thicket also does a looser comparison. It ignores names completely and slices each
    fragment's list into overlapping runs of five entries. If at least 80% of those runs show up in both
    fragments, they're <strong>near copies</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparing every run against every other fragment's runs would be slow. So thicket hashes
    each run, sorts the hashes into 128 buckets, and keeps only the smallest hash in each
    bucket. Two fragments that share most of their runs end up sharing most of those 128
    numbers, so comparing them gives an estimate within a few percent. (The trick is called MinHash, if you want to look
    it up.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Switch the demo above to <em>near-copy sketch</em> and try <em>Added a line</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why 80%? I had LLM judges rate near copies from two real codebases. They never saw thicket's
    scores. For pairs at least 80% alike, the judges said 7 in 10 were worth folding into the same fix.
    For pairs 70 to 80% alike, only 3 in 10 were.
  </p>

  <h3 id="no-chains">Near copies can form chains</h3>
  <p>
    Here's a problem with "near". Code gets copied, tweaked, and then copied again. Each version
    is near the one before it. The first and last versions may not be near each other at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below are four versions of a <code>toRow</code> function. Each one changes a line or two.
    Every version is near the one before it. But A and D are below the 80% bar.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="drift-chain-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Grouping rule">
      <button type="button" data-dchain="union" aria-pressed="true">Link every near pair</button>
      <button type="button" data-dchain="center">thicket: fixed centers</button>
    </div>
    <div class="dchain-cards"></div>
    <figcaption class="dchain-caption" aria-live="polite"></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Grouping near copies is a clustering problem. The simplest way links every near pair, and
    puts anything linked into one group, even if it's only linked through other fragments.
    That's called single-linkage clustering. Here it puts A and D in one group, because B and C
    connect them. That's a <strong>chain</strong>. On a real repo, chains ran across the whole
    codebase. 26,318 of its 65,150 distinct fragments landed in a single group.
  </p>
  <p>
    thicket breaks chains by clustering around fixed centers. The centers are findings, taken
    in rank order (section 5 explains how findings are ranked). The best-ranked finding becomes
    the first center, and it claims only what's near it directly. Then the best-ranked finding
    that's still unclaimed becomes the next center, and so on. Every member
    of a group is near its center, so a group can't stretch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the same idea with nine findings. Each dot is a finding, and bigger means
    higher-ranked. Lines connect pairs that are near each other.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="chain-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Grouping rule">
      <button type="button" data-chain="union" aria-pressed="true">Link every near pair</button>
      <button type="button" data-chain="center">thicket: fixed centers</button>
    </div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="chain-caption"></svg>
    <figcaption id="chain-caption"></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    A finding that gets claimed by a center doesn't vanish. Its copies are listed under the
    center's finding as near copies. They show up in the report but don't count toward the
    score. When the judges compared rankings with and without them counted, they preferred each
    about half the time.
  </p>

  <!-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="nesting">4. Drop the nested repeats</h2>
  <p>
    Fragments with the same hash form a finding. Right away there's a pile of overlap. If three
    functions are copies, so are their bodies, and their loops, and the blocks inside the loops.
    That's one finding reported four times.
  </p>
  <p>
    thicket keeps the biggest one and drops what sits inside it. It also cleans up between
    levels. Every exact copy is also a renamed copy, so many L1 findings just repeat an L0 one.
    thicket drops those, and keeps an L1 finding only when the renaming joins copies that L0 saw
    as different.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before thicket throws away an inner piece, it checks whether that piece also shows up
    somewhere <em>outside</em> the copies. The report lists those spots as
    <strong>same shape in other surroundings</strong>. That is often where someone already wrote
    the shared version.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="nest-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Step">
      <button type="button" data-nest="before" aria-pressed="true">Every match</button>
      <button type="button" data-nest="after">After dropping nested repeats</button>
    </div>
    <div class="nest-files"></div>
    <div class="nest-report"></div>
  </figure>

  <p>
    On one real report, 115 test files carried the same browser stub. Its <em>same shape in
    other surroundings</em> line pointed at the project's test setup file, which already had
    that stub. So the fix was deleting 115 blocks. Nobody had to write a new helper.
  </p>

  <!-- 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="ranking">5. Rank what is left</h2>
  <p>
    A 48-file repo produced about 495 duplication findings. The report shows 20 to 50. So the
    ranking matters more than anything else on this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The obvious score is "lines you would delete". That ranks 50 copies of a 4-line snippet
    above 2 copies of a 100-line function. That's backwards. Every copy you replace still costs
    an import and a call. A tiny snippet saves almost nothing per copy.
  </p>
  <p>Here's what thicket uses instead:</p>
  <p class="formula">score = size<sup>1.5</sup> × (copies − 1)<sup>k</sup></p>
  <p>
    <em>Size</em> is the syntax node count of one copy. <em>k</em> is 0.9 for exact (L0) copies
    and 0.6 for renamed (L1) ones. An exponent below 1 means each extra copy adds a bit less
    than the one before. Renamed copies get the smaller one, because each extracted copy needs
    parameters for its names.
    Last, thicket multiplies by how far apart the copies are. Copies in different modules (by default,
    a module is a directory) get 2.5, since merging them also removes a dependency between modules. Copies in different files
    of one module get 1.4, and copies in a single file get 0.8.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="rank-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Examples">
      <button type="button" data-rank="tiny">50 tiny copies vs 2 big ones</button>
      <button type="button" data-rank="rename">Exact vs renamed</button>
      <button type="button" data-rank="spread">One file vs across modules</button>
    </div>
    <div class="rank-cards"></div>
    <div class="rank-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <figcaption>
      The demo takes lines and assumes about 7 AST nodes per line. It also includes a small 0.9
      discount on renamed copies.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Those exponents weren't picked by feel. The same judges rated 243 findings from two large
    codebases, from "leave it" to "definitely merge". I scored how well each formula's top 40
    matched their ratings, using nDCG, a standard ranking metric. On a scale where 1.0
    means the top 40 lines up perfectly with the judges, lines saved got 0.48 and this formula
    gets 0.73. Anything from 1.5 to 2 for size scored about the same, so the exact number
    doesn't matter much.
  </p>

  <!-- 6 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="drift">6. Ask if merging buys anything</h2>
  <p>
    Size and count don't tell you everything. Two findings can have the same size and the same
    count, and one is worth a lot more than the other. The difference is in the
    <strong>field names</strong>: object keys, class properties, methods, enum members and JSX
    attributes.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If the field names are the same in every copy and only the values change, it's one idea
      with a few settings. A base class or a table absorbs it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the field names change from copy to copy, these are different things that happen to
      share a pattern. The only thing you could extract is a generic "copy these keys"
      helper, and nobody wants that.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    thicket works out what share of the field names change between copies, and calls that
    <strong>drift</strong>. No drift keeps the full score, and total drift cuts it to a quarter.
    Local variable names don't count. Copies that differ only in local names are exactly what
    L1 matching is for.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="drift-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Examples">
      <button type="button" data-drift="values" aria-pressed="true">Same fields, new values</button>
      <button type="button" data-drift="keys">Different fields</button>
      <button type="button" data-drift="jsx">JSX</button>
    </div>
    <div class="drift-copies"></div>
    <div class="drift-meter" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </figure>

  <p>
    JSX attributes count as field names too, and it matters. If thicket ignored them,
    <code>&lt;p role className&gt;</code> would match <code>&lt;Badge variant className&gt;</code>
    with zero drift. But that zero would only mean nothing was measured. On one app, ten of the
    top findings looked like that, and a reader threw out every one by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tag names don't count, on purpose. Four dialog headers that wrap the same title and
    description are real duplication. A finding that matches a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, a
    <code>&lt;TabsList&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;SelectContent&gt;</code> just because they share
    a layout is not. Both kinds have four or five different tag names, so counting tag names
    can't tell them apart.
  </p>

  <!-- 7 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="not-duplication">7. Skip what only looks duplicated</h2>
  <p>
    Some repetition is fine. It's the right way to write the code, or it's code nobody wrote by
    hand. These rules catch the common ones. Each rule has its own flag to turn it off.
  </p>

  <div class="rule-cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>It's already shared</h3>
      <pre><code>export async function GET(req) {
  return handle(req, { user: session.user });
}</code></pre>
      <p>
        43 route handlers that each call one shared helper look exactly like copy-paste. But
        each one is already a single call, and you can't replace a call with anything shorter.
        So thicket skips an exact match that's just one call, with no body and no values to pull
        out. Twenty copies of
        <code>useEffect(() =&gt; { … })</code> still count. The body is what you'd extract.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>It's a data table</h3>
      <pre><code>const ROUTES = {
  home:  { path: "/",  auth: false },
  inbox: { path: "/i", auth: true },
  …
};</code></pre>
      <p>
        Entries side by side in one object are the content, not a missing function. When every
        copy sits in the same object, the score drops to a fifth. And thicket never counts more
        than 10 copies of one pattern from a single file.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The framework asked for it</h3>
      <pre><code>// Button.stories.tsx, Card.stories.tsx, …
const meta = { component: Button,
  parameters: { layout: "centered" } };</code></pre>
      <p>
        Storybook wants one <code>meta</code> per story file. When the copies sit in files that
        share a suffix, like <code>.stories.tsx</code>, one per file, the score drops to a
        quarter. Compare
        a database column definition repeated seven times inside one <code>.schema.ts</code>
        file. That's not one per file, so it's real duplication and keeps its full score.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>A machine wrote it</h3>
      <pre><code>// @generated by codegen. Do not edit.</code></pre>
      <p>
        thicket checks the top of each file for a generator banner and skips files that have
        one. In one monorepo, half the files had a banner. One generated route table was holding
        a 701-file import cycle together. Without it, the real cycle was 14 files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 8 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="lanes">8. Give tests and types their own sections</h2>
  <p>
    Some kinds of duplication can't be ranked fairly against the rest. So they get their own
    section of the report, with its own cap on how many findings it shows.
  </p>
  <div class="lanes">
    <div class="lane">
      <div class="lane-head">Duplication</div>
      <div class="lane-body">Production code. Most of the slots.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="lane">
      <div class="lane-head">Duplication in tests</div>
      <div class="lane-body">Half or more of the copies are in test files.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="lane">
      <div class="lane-head">Duplicated types</div>
      <div class="lane-body">Interfaces and type aliases declared more than once.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Tests</strong> repeat themselves on purpose. Four parallel arrange-act-assert blocks
    often read better than a helper. When tests were ranked with everything else, test
    scaffolding took 10 of the top 40 spots. I tried scoring test findings lower. As I turned the
    weight down, the number of test findings in the top 40 shrank smoothly toward zero, with no
    point where it obviously looked right. A separate section avoids the question.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Types</strong> are small by nature, and any score built on size has the same
    problem with them. Measured in lines saved, a five-line interface
    pasted four times scores 13, and a typical duplicated function scores 43. Types lose every
    contest decided by size. One app had 33 groups of identical type declarations, and the
    report showed none of them. Three of those were separate logger interfaces that should have
    been one. That's real complexity, and merging them removes an idea even if it barely removes
    lines. Merging them removes an
    idea, even if it barely removes lines.
  </p>

  <!-- 9 ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <h2 id="varies">9. Say what changes between copies</h2>
  <p>
    "19 classes are the same shape" leaves the real work to the reader. When I handed that
    finding to coding agents, every one of them went and rebuilt the list of what differed by
    hand. So thicket lists it.
  </p>
  <pre><code>### THK-DUP-… · 19 copies × ~14 lines · ~220 lines recoverable

L1 · `ClassDeclaration`

- **varies across copies:** `code` (18), `unit` (4), `label` (19)</code></pre>
  <p>
    The numbers count distinct values. Read that way, it's a table with 19 rows and three
    columns, and that table is the abstraction. It also caught a real bug. 19 copies but only 18
    distinct codes meant two classes declared the same code.
  </p>
  <p>
    thicket compares up to 20 copies. If every one of those differs, it prints
    <code>≥20</code>, not <code>20</code>. On one real 103-copy finding, a bare 20 would have
    said "small, fixed set of values, build a table". In fact there were 101 different values,
    so that field has to stay a parameter.
  </p>

  <!-- 10 ----------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <h2 id="tangles">10. Untangle the imports</h2>
  <p>
    thicket also builds a graph of which modules import which. A module is a directory by
    default. It finds groups of modules where each one can reach every other by following
    imports. Those groups are called strongly connected components, and they're your tangles.
    In the diagrams below, each arrow is an <strong>edge</strong>: all the imports from one
    module into another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then it suggests a fix, cheapest first. Sometimes an import only goes through a module
    because that module re-exports something from somewhere else. Point the import at the real
    source, and the edge is gone. Nothing about the program changes. If that doesn't break the
    tangle, thicket suggests a <strong>cut</strong>: the imports you'd have to remove.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="tangle-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Step">
      <button type="button" data-tangle="before" aria-pressed="true">Before</button>
      <button type="button" data-tangle="after">Repoint the import</button>
    </div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="tangle-caption"></svg>
    <figcaption id="tangle-caption"></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 id="cuts">Finding a good cut</h3>
  <p>
    When there's nothing to repoint, thicket looks for a cut. It tries removing each edge in the
    tangle, one at a time, and measures the biggest tangle left over. The best cut is the one
    that leaves the smallest tangle. Cost only breaks ties: among cuts that leave the same
    tangle, thicket picks the one with fewer files to edit and fewer imports to move.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the cheapest edge isn't always the best one. Click edges below to cut them. The numbers
    count imports along each edge.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo" id="cut-demo">
    <div class="presets" role="group" aria-label="Actions">
      <button type="button" data-cut="cheapest">Cut the cheapest edge</button>
      <button type="button" data-cut="pick">Show thicket's pick</button>
      <button type="button" data-cut="reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="cut-caption"></svg>
    <div class="verdict" id="cut-verdict" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <figcaption id="cut-caption">
      Six modules, all tangled. Click an edge to cut it, and click again to put it back.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>A few more rules keep the suggestions honest.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Type-only imports are counted separately.</strong> <code>import type</code>
      disappears at compile time, so a cycle made only of type imports can't break anything at
      runtime. A runtime cycle can, because one module may use another's export before it's
      been defined. A type cycle still matters, since you can't understand either module without
      the other. When ignoring type imports
      changes the size of a tangle, the report gives both sizes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A cut has to matter.</strong> If the best single cut still leaves more than two
      thirds of the tangle, thicket says so and proposes nothing. It also tells you what the best
      cut would have removed, so you can see why.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Folders can fake a cycle.</strong> If <code>a/x.ts</code> imports
      <code>b/y.ts</code> and <code>b/z.ts</code> imports <code>a/w.ts</code>, the directories
      form a cycle but no file does. thicket checks the files underneath and tells you whether the
      cycle is real at the file level, or only exists because of how the folders are grouped.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="same-report">Same code, same report</h2>
  <p>
    Run thicket twice on the same code and you get the same bytes. Every list is sorted, ties
    included. Finding IDs come from the code itself, not from where it sits. So you
    can diff the report before and after a refactor and see exactly what went away.
  </p>
  <p>
    Want to see it on real output? Here's <a href="example.html">an example report</a> and
    <a href="report-guide.html">the guide to every field</a>.
  </p>
</article>
